![](/images/articles/xray.jpeg)

*Your sponsor has a meeting with the boss tomorrow to get a green-light to move forward. The next day, you get the call. "Sorry, but the boss says this isn't a priority right now. We can't get funding in this budget cycle. I want to move forward, but we'll have to hold for now."*

  

If you work in enterprise tech, you've had this experience. And you may have wondered what went wrong. You tried to be in the room, but your sponsor insisted on doing the meeting with the boss solo. You're left to wonder what happened to your most important deal.

One of my greatest learning experiences came from exactly this situation. Except, for once, I was in the room while things went sideways. I saw a conversation I normally don't get to see. It was like getting an x-ray of our deal.

I was CRO at Visier, and we were selling to one of the largest and most storied tech firms in the Bay Area. Our sponsor — the head of workforce operations — was presenting to his boss' boss — the SVP in charge of one of their big divisions. He invited our account executive and me to be there for support. There were four people in the room: customer champion, customer SVP, Visier's account exec, and me.

Our champion began a passionate discourse explaining what he could accomplish with Visier, and the analysis he could produce with unprecedented efficiency. He was eloquent and energetic, and seemed to know our product almost as well as we did. "Working with Visier," he concluded, "will let me produce all of this information and *save me an unbelievable amount of time*."

The room fell silent. The SVP tented his fingertips Montgomery Burns-style. "I like it when you save time," he said quietly. "But it's not a priority for me to spend money on that."

I like it when you save time. But it's not a priority for me to spend money on that.

This is what happens behind closed doors. The language of your (energetic, passionate, smart, committed) sponsor simply doesn't align with what funding executives care about. They may be passionate and knowledgeable advocates, and they may know the people and politics in their organization. *But they don't know how to sell internally.*

I advise sales reps to speak the language of the business from Day One. Sometimes I get pushback that "my sponsor doesn't care about this — they just care about saving time or doing things better or [insert parochial concern here]." But there are two reasons to do it anyway.

1.  *You're sent to the people you sound like*, so you need to speak the language of the business to earn access to business owners.
2.  You want to *get your champions hearing that language so they'll repeat it later.* Part of what you're doing is turning your contacts into advocates who know how to talk about the solution when you're not in the room.

In this case, we *were* in the room, and we were able to salvage the deal. "Everything you just heard is true," I said to the SVP, "but it's not why we're here. We're here because the data we can produce can show you the characteristics of your highest performing sales reps, so you can hire and retain better people. We're here because we can show you what training courses correlate to better performance and business outcomes. *We're here to help you build a better team that delivers more revenue.*"

This time, the answer came faster. "Ok, that's worth spending money on," the SVP replied. "Now you have my attention." The meeting continued, and the deal lived on.

*That's* worth spending money on. You have my attention.

Give yourself the CFO test: If your sponsor has just two minutes to explain to the CFO why they need money to buy your software *now*, will they know what to say? Unless you've been working with them for many weeks to get there, I'd bet *not yet*. And there's a reason to improve your deal's health without an x-ray.
